Prompt Compression & Context Pruning: Defeating Token Inflation
Yesterday, learned how to built Agentic RAG to dynamically route, grade, and self-correct my retrieval context at runtime.
Today, learning about tackling the financial and performance wall of advanced AI systems: Token Inflation. As my multi-turn conversation logs, GraphRAG maps, and multi-source chunks merge, my prompt context window swells to thousands of tokens. 
Focusing on learning Prompt Compression & Context Pruning—surgically stripping away linguistic redundancy to cut context sizes by up to 50% without degrading model accuracy.

The Big Idea:
In .NET web development, passing giant, unoptimized payloads back and forth across network layers destroys system throughput. LLMs suffer from this exact same issue, but with a massive financial penalty attached.
When running stateful multi-turn agents or dense Agentic RAG systems, your prompt payload grows exponentially on every single turn. This creates two critical engineering hazards:
-The Cost & Latency Wall: Processing thousands of redundant text tokens drastically spikes time-to-first-token (TTFT) latency and drives up API execution fees.
-The "Lost in the Middle" Degradation: Even if an LLM has a large context window, its internal attention layers inherently favor the absolute beginning and the absolute end of the prompt payload. If your context is bloated with fluffy language, vital system details buried in the middle get ignored.
Prompt Compression fixes this by evaluating information density. It uses linguistic algorithms or tiny information-theoretic models to strip away low-value words (like filler phrases, redundant adverbs, and repetitive structural formatting), keeping only the dense technical semantic core before passing the payload to the main LLM.

Three Core Pruning Strategies:
1. Token-Frequency Pruning (LLMLingua Pattern)
Uses a small, lightning-fast language model (like a quantized 1B parameter model) to calculate the perplexity (surprise factor) of each token in your context. Tokens that have low perplexity (highly predictable filler text like "please note that the following information shows that") are stripped out, while high-perplexity tokens (like specific error codes, metrics, or variable values) are locked in place.
2. Sliding Window Structural TruncationInstead of storing a multi-turn chat ledger infinitely, you implement a strict sliding window memory pattern. You preserve the original immutable system instruction and the last $N$ turns of dialogue. For everything in the middle, you force an asynchronous background step to generate an absolute micro-summary, replacing hundreds of raw conversational tokens with a single dense summary paragraph.
3. Semantic Element De-duplication
When blending results from Hybrid Search and GraphRAG, different text chunks often repeat identical system declarations or boilerplate error headers. You implement string hash filters to deduplicate identical structural snippets before compilation.

The Code:
Here is how to construct a highly performant, programmatic Sliding-Window Context Pruner with historical summary fallback loops inside your .NET chat state management class:

using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;
using System.Text;

public class HighThroughputMemoryManager
{
    private readonly IChatClient _summarizerClient;
    private readonly List<ChatMessage> _activeHistory = new();
    private const int MaxConversationalTurns = 4; // Hard limit for raw message retaining

    public HighThroughputMemoryManager(IChatClient summarizerClient)
    {
        _summarizerClient = summarizerClient;
    }

    public void AddMessage(ChatRole role, string content)
    {
        _activeHistory.Add(new ChatMessage(role, content));
    }

    public async Task<List<ChatMessage>> GetPrunedContextAsync(string systemInstruction)
    {
        var optimizedPayload = new List<ChatMessage>
        {
            new ChatMessage(ChatRole.System, systemInstruction)
        };

        // If history is small, pass it through without taking a performance hit
        if (_activeHistory.Count <= MaxConversationalTurns)
        {
            optimizedPayload.AddRange(_activeHistory);
            return optimizedPayload;
        }

        // SLIDING WINDOW + MICRO-SUMMARIZATION ARCHITECTURE
        Console.WriteLine($"[Memory Management] -> History length ({_activeHistory.Count}) exceeds maximum bounds. Pruning cold context...");

        // Split history into cold turns (to be compressed) and hot turns (to be kept raw)
        int splitIndex = _activeHistory.Count - MaxConversationalTurns;
        var coldTurns = _activeHistory.Take(splitIndex).ToList();
        var hotTurns = _activeHistory.Skip(splitIndex).ToList();

        // Compress the cold history into a single dense summary block
        string condensedSummary = await CondenseHistoryAsync(coldTurns);

        // Inject the summary as historical anchor context, then append active hot turns
        optimizedPayload.Add(new ChatMessage(ChatRole.System, $"Summary of preceding conversation: {condensedSummary}"));
        optimizedPayload.AddRange(hotTurns);

        return optimizedPayload;
    }

    private async Task<string> CondenseHistoryAsync(List<ChatMessage> coldMessages)
    {
        var transcriptBuilder = new StringBuilder();
        foreach (var msg in coldMessages)
        {
            transcriptBuilder.AppendLine($"{msg.Role}: {msg.Text}");
        }

        // Use a highly restricted token constraint for a fast, flat summary generation
        var options = new ChatOptions { MaxOutputTokens = 150 };
        var response = await _summarizerClient.CompleteAsync(
            $"Condense this conversation into a dense, flat technical summary. Extract only core facts and technical metrics:\n{transcriptBuilder}", 
            options
        );

        return response.Message.Text.Trim();
    }
}

Key Takeaways:
Linguistic vs. Semantic Value: Natural language is packed with structural fluff required for human readability but useless to a multi-billion parameter model. Pruning strips the fluff, turning your context into an information-dense prompt asset.
Preserving Context Anchors: Never truncate or compress your base System prompt instruction. The system core rules must remain perfectly intact; only application search fragments and trailing conversational memory lines should be subjected to compression algorithms.
Summarization as a Background Worker: Generating summaries consumes time. In high-velocity systems, do not summarize inline with the user's active click execution flow. Fire off the summarization step asynchronously as a separate worker process immediately after a response turn completes.

